WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.920
Good morning, John.

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:02.280
Happy Pizzamas.

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:06.720
I know I’m still on paternity leave, but
I made this video early because I felt really

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:09.530
bad that I wasn’t gonna be able to make
a Pizzamas video.

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:13.760
Right now I’m walking to a place where a
bunch of people who I like make a bunch of

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.440
stuff that I like, and I wanted to show it
off.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.600
It’s Pizzamas specific; I don’t know why
I’m trying to be, like, oblique.

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:21.470
You’re gonna find out right now.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:30.720
This is Zoo City Apparel.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.800
They’ve been printing shirts for us for
almost three years now, and they print pretty

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.980
much every shirt that we sell at DFTBA.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:39.620
I asked them if I could go visit during this
year’s Pizzamas printing, and they graciously

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:42.730
accepted, and even, amazingly, provided me
with pizza.

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:44.950
So let’s meet some of these people.

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:46.450
This is Chris. He owns Zoo City.

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:47.870
Hank: Why did you start a print shop?

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:48.670
Chris: I have no idea.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:50.600
Hank: How long ago was that now?

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:51.739
Chris: Ten years.

00:00:51.739 --> 00:00:52.719
Hank: Oh my God.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:53.600
This is Lou.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:54.680
He’s one of the press operators.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.400
Lou: I started in my garage when I was seventeen.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.980
I went to school for graphic design, then
it took twenty years for it to come back around.

00:01:01.989 --> 00:01:04.779
I’m gonna go ahead and send it around, just
this shirt

00:01:04.779 --> 00:01:05.419
Hank: Yeah.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:08.980
Lou: And if you want to walk around, it’ll
go through every head, and it’ll print just this shirt.

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:09.800
Hank: Right.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:15.600
Lou: It’s gonna look completely different
when it comes out the back.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:16.460
This is Cory.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:17.760
He’s the ink guy.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:19.480
Hank: Do you listen to podcasts while you’re
doing this?

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:20.640
Cory: Oh yeah. Oh yeah.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.540
I was actually listening to yours before you
walked in here, so.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:24.180
Hank: Oh that’s crazy.

00:01:24.189 --> 00:01:24.829
Cory: Yeah.

00:01:24.829 --> 00:01:26.740
Printing a shirt isn’t like printing from
a printer.

00:01:26.749 --> 00:01:28.189
The colors get made.

00:01:28.189 --> 00:01:30.140
The inks get mixed by Cory.

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:34.200
Sometimes he has a formula to start with,
but he usually ends up eyeballing it, and

00:01:34.209 --> 00:01:37.450
the color ends up looking different on the
shirt than it does in the bucket, so it’s

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:39.659
a skill that can only be developed with time.

00:01:39.659 --> 00:01:42.700
We work with Zoo City because they make a
fantastic product.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:46.770
They care about making our shirts fantastic,
and they know how to get it done.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:50.229
I love watching them utilize their skills
to do great work, and I’m very glad that

00:01:50.229 --> 00:01:54.419
they were okay with me jumping in their space
to appreciate them making our stuff.

00:01:54.419 --> 00:01:57.709
I think, for the most part, businesses want
you to imagine that the things that you buy

00:01:57.709 --> 00:02:03.389
are made in Star Trek style replicators because
that’s simple and they don’t want to complicate

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:04.469
the transaction.

00:02:04.469 --> 00:02:07.029
They want you to think about the thing, not
the people who made it.

00:02:07.029 --> 00:02:11.709
Not necessarily because those people aren’t
happy in their jobs, just because it complicates things.

00:02:11.709 --> 00:02:15.769
But I think it’s nice, and also maybe important,
to see a bit of the complexity and the hard

00:02:15.769 --> 00:02:19.709
work that goes into the things that I buy
and the things that I sell.

00:02:19.709 --> 00:02:24.340
It might not be simple, but I think it’s
more rewarding, and it’s definitely more real.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:29.000
And frankly, it’s too easy to never appreciate
all of the people who work hard making their

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.720
lives and everyone else’s lives a little
bit better every day.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:33.840
John, happy Pizzamas.

00:02:33.849 --> 00:02:38.569
I’ll see you tomorrow, and you will see
me when I get back from paternity leave.

